-- A demand that unfolds without end (scripts/whycheck.ts): `up` is never
-- materialized, and `up(1)` asks `up(2)` for ever. The evaluation never asks
-- it — the planner holds the ground `up(1)` until `k(X)` binds, and `k` is
-- empty — but `whynot r(a)` walks the body in order and does, so it meets
-- the budget wall while demonstrating, and the answer is the wall,
-- `holds: false` (docs/port-surface.md). Not a world `npm test` loads:
-- whycheck asks it of both engines' explainers.
edb(k).
up(X) :- Y is X + 1, up(Y).
q(a).
q(b).
r(X) :- q(X), not k(X), up(1), k(X).
s(X) :- q(X).
